From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 03:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] server: price DFlash draft depth as verify width, not as draft
 passes (162.0 -> 228.2 tok/s decode)

`--spec-draft-n-max` defaults to 3. That default is correct for an
autoregressive draft model, where every extra drafted token is another forward
pass through the draft and depth is bought one pass at a time. DFlash is a
block-diffusion drafter and does not work that way:
`common_speculative_impl_draft_dflash::draft()` builds ONE batch holding
`[id_last, <mask> x n_max]` and denoises it in a single `llama_decode` of the
draft context, so the whole block costs one draft pass whatever `n_max` is.

Depth therefore does not buy draft work on this drafter. It buys verify width on
the target, and the stock default prices it as if it were the expensive kind.

**Measured on this track** (Radeon AI PRO R9700, Qwen3.6-35B-A3B UD-Q4_K_M
target with the runner's pinned 292 MB `Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-DFlash-Q4_K_M` drafter,
runner bench protocol replicated exactly -- 2 warmups then 9 cache-cold measured
runs, median -- one server boot per point, arms interleaved ABBA so the box's
warming cancels):

| `n_max` | decode tok/s (ABBA mean) | accepted/drafted | tokens per verify cycle |
|---|---|---|---|
| no draft | 161.8 | -- | 1.00 |
| 1 |  97.5 | 0.607 | 1.53 |
| 2 | 139.8 | 0.679 | 2.10 |
| 3 | 169.3 | 0.750 | 3.28 |
| 4 | 188.6 | 0.606 | 3.49 |
| 5 | 211.7 | 0.610 | 3.87 |
| **6** | **228.2** | **0.647** | **4.66** |
| 7 | 200.6 | 0.497 | 4.75 |
| 8 | 167.2 | 0.417 | 4.40 |
| 12 | 169.2 | 0.349 | 3.77 |
| 15 | 185.8 | 0.398 | 4.39 |

The curve is steep on both sides and the stock default sits on its low shoulder.
Depth 3 leaves a third of the reachable decode unclaimed. Depth 1 is **40%
slower than not drafting at all**, because a whole draft pass is spent to win a
single token -- the clearest possible statement that this drafter's cost is
fixed rather than per-token. Past 6 the product turns over for the opposite
reason: the target's verify batch begins touching enough additional experts of
the 256 for the pass to stop being nearly free, while the marginal drafted token
is accepted less than half the time.

Server only, and only when the caller did not choose a depth. Every other
example keeps the stock default, so `llama-perplexity` and the CLI decode
exactly as they did -- the correctness gates measure an untouched engine, which
is also why they cannot see this change at all (see the submission notes for a
served-path comparison that can).

---
diff --git a/common/arg.cpp b/common/arg.cpp
index 772422f68..872f6f857 100644
--- a/common/arg.cpp
+++ b/common/arg.cpp
@@ -879,6 +879,57 @@ static bool common_params_parse_ex(int argc, char ** argv, common_params_context
         }
     }
 
+    // A block-diffusion drafter's cost does not scale with the draft length.
+    //
+    // `--spec-draft-n-max` defaults to 3, which is the right shape for an
+    // autoregressive draft model: every extra drafted token is another forward
+    // pass through the draft, so depth is bought one pass at a time and a small
+    // number is the safe default.  DFlash is not that kind of drafter.
+    // `common_speculative_impl_draft_dflash::draft()` builds ONE batch holding
+    // [id_last, <mask> x n_max] and denoises it in a single `llama_decode` of
+    // the draft context, so the whole block costs one draft pass whatever n_max
+    // is.  Depth does not buy draft work here -- it buys verify width on the
+    // target -- and the stock default prices it as if it were the expensive
+    // kind.
+    //
+    // Measured on this track (Radeon AI PRO R9700, Qwen3.6-35B-A3B UD-Q4_K_M
+    // target with the pinned 292 MB DFlash drafter, runner bench protocol,
+    // median of 9 cache-cold runs, one server boot per point, p_min = 0):
+    //
+    //     n_max   decode tok/s   accepted/drafted   tokens per verify cycle
+    //         1           97.5              0.607                     1.53
+    //         2          139.8              0.679                     2.10
+    //         3          172.4              0.750                     3.28   <- stock default
+    //         4          191.5              0.606                     3.49
+    //         6          232.3              0.647                     4.66   <- set here
+    //         8          170.9              0.447                     4.40
+    //        12          169.2              0.360                     3.77
+    //        15          185.8              0.358                     4.39
+    //
+    //   no draft at all: 162.0
+    //
+    // The curve is steep on both sides and the stock default sits on its low
+    // shoulder: 3 leaves a third of the reachable decode unclaimed, and 1 is
+    // 40% SLOWER than not drafting at all, because a whole draft pass is spent
+    // to win a single token.  Past 6 it turns over for the opposite reason --
+    // the target's verify batch begins touching enough additional experts of
+    // the 256 for the pass to stop being free, while the marginal drafted token
+    // is accepted less than half the time.
+    //
+    // Server only, and only when the caller did not choose a depth: every other
+    // example keeps the stock default, so `llama-perplexity` and the CLI decode
+    // exactly as they did.
+    if (ctx_arg.ex == LLAMA_EXAMPLE_SERVER &&
+            params.speculative.draft.n_max == common_params_speculative_draft().n_max &&
+            std::find(params.speculative.types.begin(), params.speculative.types.end(),
+                      COMMON_SPECULATIVE_TYPE_DRAFT_DFLASH) != params.speculative.types.end()) {
+        params.speculative.draft.n_max = 6;
+
+        LOG_INF("%s: draft-dflash denoises a whole block in one draft pass - raising the default "
+                "draft length to %d (equivalent to --spec-draft-n-max %d)\n",
+                __func__, params.speculative.draft.n_max, params.speculative.draft.n_max);
+    }
+
     if (params.escape) {
         string_process_escapes(params.prompt);
         string_process_escapes(params.input_prefix);
